    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d798">

                <pb facs="0729" n="645" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg645"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72114/3175</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-12T11:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 12, 1918, 11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 6.45 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8243. Foreign Office reports that in view of present undesirability of
                    facilitating supplies for Russian prisoners of war in Germany, the suggestion
                    has been made that British censor detain correspondence <pb facs="0730" n="646"
                        xml:id="pg646"/> contained in transit mail relating to provision of food or
                    money for them. Government here rather doubtful whether such drastic action is
                    desirable or necessary and would be glad to have your opinion on this point.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
